> This guide is the single source of truth for *how* to build what the epic and architecture specify. Follow tasks in order. Tasks 1–3 are parallel-safe; Task 4 blocks on all three. Task 5 closes the epic.

---

## Prerequisites

Before writing a line of HTML, resolve these open questions from the analysis:

| Blocker | Owner | Needed By |
|---------|-------|-----------|
| Specview auth URL (production signup/login endpoint) | Specview team | Before Task 4 |
| Stripe $29/month checkout URL | You (Task 1) | Before Task 4 |
| Confirm what "3 projects/month" meters (created vs. specs generated, monthly reset vs. cap) | Product | Before Task 3 copy is finalised |
| Pricing section placement in the page (between hero and self-host, or below self-host) | Design | Before Task 3 |

Until the Specview auth URL is known, use `https://app.specview.io/signup` as a placeholder and mark it with an `<!-- AUTH_URL -->` comment. Until Stripe checkout is live, use `#stripe-tbd` with a `<!-- STRIPE_URL -->` comment. These are the only two config strings the whole page needs.

---

## File Map

```
landing/
└── index.html          ← the only file you touch
```

All CSS lives in `<style>` or the linked stylesheet already loaded by `index.html`. Do not create new files. Do not add new `<link rel="stylesheet">` tags. Do not add `<script>` tags.

---

## Task 1 — Stripe Pro Product Setup

**Effort**: 0.5 days | **Parallel with**: Tasks 2 and 3

### Steps

1. Log into the Stripe Dashboard → Products → **+ Add product**.
2. Name: `Specview Pro`
3. Pricing model: **Recurring** — $29.00 USD / month.
4. Save. Stripe creates a Price ID (looks like `price_1ABC...`).
5. Navigate to **Payment Links** → **+ New** → select the Specview Pro price.
6. Enable **hosted checkout** (default). Set success URL to `https://app.specview.io/welcome` (or wherever Specview routes post-payment).
7. Copy the generated Payment Link URL. It looks like:
   ```
   https://buy.stripe.com/xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx
   ```
8. Drop this URL into a shared doc or env config — it will be pasted into the HTML in Task 4.

### Done When

- [ ] Product "Specview Pro" exists in Stripe Dashboard.
- [ ] Recurring $29/month price is attached.
- [ ] A Stripe Payment Link URL is recorded and ready to paste.
- [ ] No Stripe SDK or API key is anywhere near `landing/`.

---

## Task 2 — Hero Dual-CTA Block

**Effort**: 1 day | **Parallel with**: Tasks 1 and 3

### Context

The current hero has a single CTA. You are replacing that slot with a two-button row. The hero's existing grid (`1fr 1px 340px`) stays intact — only the CTA element inside the lede column changes.

### HTML — Locate and Replace the Existing CTA Slot

Find the current single-CTA element in `landing/index.html` (look for the hero section's action/button area). Replace it with:

```html
<!-- Hero CTA Row -->
<div class="hero-cta-row">
  <a
    href="https://app.specview.io/signup"
    class="btn btn--primary"
    <!-- AUTH_URL — update both occurrences when Specview auth URL is confirmed -->
  >Try it free</a>
  <a
    href="#self-host"
    class="btn btn--secondary"
  >Self-host ↓</a>
</div>
```

> The `href="#self-host"` assumes the existing self-host section has `id="self-host"`. Verify this in the current HTML and adjust the id if needed — do not add a new anchor, use what's already there.

### CSS — Add to Existing Stylesheet Block

```css
/* ── Hero CTA Row ───────────────────────────── */
.hero-cta-row {
  display: flex;
  gap: 12px;
  align-items: center;
  flex-wrap: wrap;           /* wraps at mobile without a media query */
}

.btn {
  display: inline-block;
  padding: 10px 24px;
  font-family: var(--font-sans);   /* Source Sans 3 */
  font-size: 13px;
  font-weight: 600;
  letter-spacing: 0.04em;
  text-transform: uppercase;
  text-decoration: none;
  border-radius: 0;                /* system rule: no radius on buttons */
  cursor: pointer;
  transition: background 0.1s ease, color 0.1s ease;
}

.btn--primary {
  background: var(--ink);
  color: var(--bg);
  border: 2px solid var(--ink);
}

.btn--primary:hover {
  background: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.85);
}

.btn--secondary {
  background: transparent;
  color: var(--ink);
  border: 2px solid var(--ink);
}

.btn--secondary:hover {
  background: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.02);   /* system whisper */
}
```

### Smooth Scroll

Add `scroll-behavior: smooth` to the `html` selector in the existing stylesheet if it isn't already there:

```css
html {
  scroll-behavior: smooth;
}
```

No JavaScript. No scroll library.

### Done When

- [ ] Hero shows exactly two CTAs side by side on desktop.
- [ ] "Try it free" href points to the auth URL placeholder (marked with `<!-- AUTH_URL -->`).
- [ ] "Self-host" href is a same-page hash anchor that scrolls to the existing self-host section.
- [ ] Both buttons are square-cornered (no border-radius), border-outlined per Newspaper Design System.
- [ ] No new fonts, no new stylesheet files, no `<script>` tags introduced.

